# 0005. ADR before code, granular ADRs, one ADR per PR

- Status: Accepted
- Date: 2026-11-11

Decided by the owner on 2026-11-11: every feature or major change starts with an approved
ADR, one decision per ADR, implemented in its own PR.

## Technical details
- **Problem**: Claude could start coding a feature before the decision behind it was recorded
  and approved, and one PR could mix several decisions, which made review and history hard
  to follow.
- **ADR first**: in any project with a codebase, every feature or major change starts with an
  ADR (`adr-manager`). No code, scaffolding or config for it until I approve the ADR
  (`Accepted`) and give the go; Claude shows the draft and stops.
- **Granular**: one decision per ADR; a feature needing several decisions gets several ADRs.
  If B depends on A, A gets the lower number and is implemented first.
- **One ADR, one PR**: the PR carries the ADR and its implementation, nothing else
  (`github-flow`). Its squash commit on `main` is tagged `ADR-NNNN`.
- **Commits**: every subject starts with the ADR it falls under (`commit-style`): a major
  change uses its new number (`[ADR-0011]`); a minor change (fix, docs, tweak) needs no new
  ADR and uses the latest number plus a minor version (`[ADR-0010.3]`), found with
  `git log --format=%s | grep -o '\[ADR-0010\.[0-9]*\]' | head -1`.
- **Files**: `docs/adr/NNNN-short-title.adr`, index in `docs/adr/README.md`.
- **Consequences**: every major change has an approved record and history reads in
  dependency order; the cost is an approval round before coding and more, smaller PRs.
  Only I bring changes into `main` ([0003](0003-only-the-owner-merges-to-main.adr)).

## Technologies and alternatives
- Alternatives: ADRs only for big decisions, written after the code: the record follows the
  code instead of steering it; one ADR per feature with many decisions: hard to supersede
  one part.
